":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/data/measured#phone-class-estimates","content":"heap peak while deriving SUBSTRING · 55.00 MiB · 64 MiB","table":"table-5"},"734":{"id":"/docs/getting-started","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"page","content":"Quickstart","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Getting started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started"},"735":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-0","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started","content":"Download a pack, run your first queries from the command line, then from your language."},"736":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-1","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started#get-a-pack","content":"Get a pack"},"737":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-2","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started#install-the-command-line","content":"Install the command line"},"738":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-3","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started#ask-it-something","content":"Ask it something"},"739":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-4","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"Use it from code"},"740":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-5","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"What to read next"},"741":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-6","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started","content":"This page takes you from nothing to answers in a few minutes: a pack, the `tgd` command line to\nlook inside it, and the same queries from code."},"742":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-7","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#get-a-pack","content":"A pack is one file. The world lite pack holds the world's countries, first-level units and\ncities of 5,000 or more, India's districts and statutory towns, and Nepal in full: 172,339\nplaces named in Nepali, Hindi and English, with heights, admin units, timezones and boundaries."},"743":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-8","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#get-a-pack","content":"It is 15.6 MB on disk and 9.8 MB compressed on the wire, under CC BY 4.0: you may ship it in an\napp, with the credits it carries."},"744":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-9","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#install-the-command-line","content":"`tgd` inspects, verifies and queries packs, and serves them over HTTP and MCP. Install it from\ncrates.io (a Rust toolchain is all it needs):"},"745":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-10","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#ask-it-something","content":"The first is the city (a settlement), the second the metropolitan municipality that holds it\n(an admin unit at level 3), the third a smaller settlement of the same name farther west. `tgd:np/1xhb` is\nthe city's stable ID. From a point:"},"746":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-11","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#ask-it-something","content":"The command-line guide shows every query."},"747":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-12","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"Install the package for your language, then follow the\nguides, which open this same pack and run every query with its output in each language:"},"748":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-13","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"<Card title=\"Open a pack\" href=\"/docs/guides/open\" />"},"749":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-14","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"<Card title=\"Search by name\" href=\"/docs/guides/search\" />"},"750":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-15","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"<Card title=\"Reverse geocoding\" href=\"/docs/guides/reverse\" />"},"751":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-16","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"<Card title=\"Timezones\" href=\"/docs/guides/timezones\" />"},"752":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-17","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#use-it-from-code","content":"The search above, from code:"},"753":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-18","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Places and IDs: what a place is, its kinds and levels, and why IDs\nstay stable."},"754":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-19","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Packs and components: what is inside a pack and how to ship only the\nparts you use."},"755":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-20","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Results: the columns every query returns and how to read them fast."},"756":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-21","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Licences: what you must credit, and where."},"757":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","type":"page","content":"Install","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Getting started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/install"},"758":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-0","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install","content":"Add teigeodata to a project in your language, and read the results a query returns."},"759":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-1","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Add the package"},"760":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-2","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/install#get-a-pack","content":"Get a pack"},"761":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-3","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Read a result"},"762":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-4","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/install#names-in-your-language","content":"Names in your language"},"763":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-5","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/getting-started/install#next","content":"Next"},"764":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-6","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install","content":"teigeodata is one Rust engine with a binding for each language, generated from one\ndefinition: the calls, their arguments and the columns they return are the same everywhere.\nPick your language; every page of these docs remembers it."},"765":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-7","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"teigeodata is written in Rust, and Rust gets the engine itself: typed places, typed IDs and\npoints, and queries that write into buffers you reuse, so a warm query allocates nothing. Every\nother language reaches the same engine through a generated binding."},"766":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-8","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The crates on crates.io: `teigeodata-core`, `-engine`, `-sync` (the store), `-api`, `-server`,\n`-cli` (`tgd`) and what they need, all at the same version."},"767":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-9","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The minimum Rust version is the one `rust-toolchain.toml` pins. `teigeodata-engine`'s default\nfeatures read packs from files (`mmap`); `teigeodata-sync` adds the store (download, verify,\nupdate); the core crates build for `no_std` targets."},"768":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-10","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The Python package is a native extension (PyO3) over the Rust engine, with type stubs. One\n`abi3` wheel per platform serves CPython 3.11 and later; free-threaded builds (3.14t, 3.15t) get\ntheir own wheels and run queries in parallel without the GIL."},"769":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-11","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Wheels for Linux (glibc and musl), macOS and Windows on x64 and Arm64."},"770":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-12","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"771":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-13","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Or build a wheel with maturin: `pip install ./bindings/python`."},"772":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-14","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"`teigeodata` is a native addon (N-API, napi-rs layout) with TypeScript types: one\nprebuilt addon per platform, installed as an optional dependency, so `npm install` downloads only\nyours. It runs on Node.js, and on Bun and Deno through their N-API support. For browsers and\nedge workers use the WebAssembly package (the **Browser** tab), which has the same API."},"773":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-15","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"npm installs the native addon for your platform with it (`@teispace/teigeodata-<platform>`)."},"774":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-16","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"775":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-17","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"`cargo xtask package-node` makes the packages as they are published, under `target/node/`."},"776":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-18","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Node.js 22 or later. The package is ESM and CommonJS (`require` works too)."},"777":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-19","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"`teigeodata-wasm` is the engine compiled to WebAssembly, for browsers, Web Workers,\nDeno, Bun, Node.js and Cloudflare Workers. The API is the Node.js package's (the **Node.js** tab);\nthe difference is where packs come from: a page has no file system, so it opens a pack from its\nbytes. After that one download, every query runs locally. The playground is this\npackage in a page: every query on the pack below, with the code each answer came from."},"778":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-20","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"779":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-21","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Two packages, the same API. The full module is at most 256 KiB gzipped (a CI gate); the\n`-lookup` one leaves out name search and is at most 150 KiB, for apps that only reverse geocode,\nread timezones or look places up by ID."},"780":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-22","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The Swift package wraps the C library: on Apple platforms it links an XCFramework (iOS,\nthe iOS simulators, macOS), on Linux the library you build. Swift 6 with typed throws; every\nhandle is `Sendable`."},"781":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-23","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The release tags are the Swift releases; each one fetches the release's XCFramework and checks\nits checksum:"},"782":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-24","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"In Xcode: File › Add Package Dependencies, and the repository URL."},"783":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-25","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"784":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-26","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"and depend on `bindings/swift` by path, linking `target/release` as the tour's\nPackage.swift\ndoes. `cargo xtask package-swift` makes the XCFramework the release ships."},"785":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-27","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"iOS 15, macOS 12, and Linux."},"786":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-28","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"One library serves Kotlin and Java, on desktop JVMs (Java 17 or later) and on Android (API 24\nor later). It calls the native library through JNI; the JAR carries it for each desktop\nplatform, the AAR for each Android ABI (arm64-v8a, armeabi-v7a, x86_64, 16 KB page aligned)."},"787":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-29","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"On Maven Central:"},"788":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-30","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"789":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-31","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"One library serves Kotlin and Java, on desktop JVMs (Java 17 or later) and on Android (API 24\nor later). It calls the native library through JNI; the JAR carries it for each desktop\nplatform, the AAR for each Android ABI (arm64-v8a, armeabi-v7a, x86_64, 16 KB page aligned)."},"790":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-32","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"On Maven Central:"},"791":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-33","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"792":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-34","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"One package for Dart and Flutter on Android, iOS, macOS, Linux, Windows and the web. Natively\nit calls the C library through FFI, supplied by the package's build hook; on the web (dart2js or\ndart2wasm) the same classes run on the WebAssembly module."},"793":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-35","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build the library with cargo instead of downloading it, point the build hook at a checkout:"},"794":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-36","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Dart 3.10 or later (Flutter 3.38 or later). The build hook fetches the prebuilt library for the\ntarget and checks it against the SHA-256 the package pins; `library:` names one you built,\n`prebuilt_base:` a mirror."},"795":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-37","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The Go module calls the C library through cgo, or through\npurego when built with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, so it cross-compiles\nwithout a C toolchain. The library is loaded at run time: the file passed to `LoadLibrary`, then\nthe one `TEIGEODATA_LIB` names, then the release's prebuilt library in the user cache (checked\nagainst the SHA-256 the module pins), then the system's."},"796":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-38","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The module's versions are the tags `bindings/go/vX.Y.Z`, made with each release. Go 1.26 or\nlater. Fetch the release's prebuilt library once:"},"797":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-39","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"or build it from a checkout and name it:"},"798":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-40","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"The C library is the ABI every other binding builds on: plain functions, opaque handles,\nstatus codes, and no global state but each thread's last error. Its header is checked against the\nlast release's on every change, so a minor release never breaks a program built against it."},"799":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-41","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"From the release: unpack `teigeodata-c-<version>-<target>.tar.gz`, which holds the headers,\nthe shared and static libraries, a pkg-config file and a CMake package."},"800":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-42","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"801":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-43","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"`teigeodata.hpp` is a header-only C++17 layer over the C library: RAII handles, `std::string_view`\narguments, exceptions with the stable error codes, and `teigeodata_reader.hpp` to read results\nwith a range-based `for`. Everything is in namespace `teigeodata`; the tour shortens it with\n`namespace tg = teigeodata;`."},"802":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-44","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Install the C package (the **C** tab): the C++ headers ship in it. Then\ncompile as C++17 or later:"},"803":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-45","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"`Teispace.TeiGeodata` is the .NET binding: source-generated P/Invoke (`LibraryImport`) with\nno marshalling at run time, compatible with NativeAOT and trimming. The package carries the\nnative library for Windows, macOS and Linux (glibc and musl) on x64 and Arm64."},"804":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-46","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Not on NuGet yet. Once it is:"},"805":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-47","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"To build from a checkout instead (a Rust toolchain is all it needs;\n`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the version):"},"806":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-48","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"and add `target/dotnet` as a package source, or reference\n`bindings/dotnet/TeiGeodata/TeiGeodata.csproj` as the tour's project does."},"807":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-49","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":".NET 10 or later."},"808":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-50","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#add-the-package","content":"Every release is published to crates.io, PyPI, npm, Maven Central, pub.dev and the Go module\nproxy from CI, by trusted publishing, with provenance where the registry records it. Swift\ninstalls from the release tags; C from the release archives. The .NET package is built and\ntested but not yet on NuGet: build it from a checkout."},"809":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-51","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#get-a-pack","content":"Queries read **packs**: signed, memory-mapped files of places. The examples use the world\nlite pack: the world's countries, first-level units and cities of 5,000 or more, India's\ndistricts and statutory towns, and Nepal in full, 172,339 places named in Nepali, Hindi and\nEnglish (15.6 MB, CC BY 4.0):"},"810":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-52","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#get-a-pack","content":"Which pack to choose, and how an app ships or downloads one: packs and\nthe store."},"811":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-53","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns its rows as one **result blob**: compact columns in a single buffer,\nread in place without copying. Each language's reader turns a row into its own natural type\nand a null into its own null:"},"812":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-54","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"The Rust engine returns typed values, not result blobs: a `Place` holds its ID, kind, admin\nlevel and point, and the rest (its name, ISO code, elevation, timezone) is read on demand from\nthe engine, into a buffer you pass. The tour gathers what it prints in one helper:"},"813":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-55","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Queries that return several places write into a `Vec` you own and reuse (`hits`, `near`,\n`places` in the tour), with a `Scratch` for the search's working memory. Points are fixed-point\n(`Point<E6>`, `Point<E7>`): `Point::from_degrees` checks the range once, so a query never sees\nan invalid coordinate."},"814":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-56","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"If you want the same blobs every binding gets, `teigeodata-api` wraps this engine with the\nbinding API (`Engine::search` returning a result blob)."},"815":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-57","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob**: the rows as compact columns in one `Blob`, a buffer the\nlibrary lends without a copy (`memoryview(blob)`; `bytes(blob)` copies it), read in place. `teigeodata.blob.Reader` reads it; iterating gives each row as a `dict` from column\nname to value, with `None` where a value is null:"},"816":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-58","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"For large results read whole columns instead: `reader.numbers(\"lat\")` is a `memoryview` of the\nblob (no copy),\n`reader.strings(\"name\")` a list, `reader.nulls(\"elevation\")` which rows are null, and\n`reader.column(\"level\")` one value per row. `reader.rows` and `reader.columns` describe the\nshape. The import:"},"817":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-59","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (a `Buffer`): the rows as compact columns, read in\nplace. `Reader` reads it, and iterating gives each row as a plain object, with `null` where a\nvalue is null:"},"818":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-60","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"For large results read whole columns: `reader.numbers(\"lat\")` is a typed array of the column's\nown type (a `Float64Array` for `lat`) viewing the blob, `reader.strings(\"name\")` an array,\n`reader.nulls(\"elevation\")` which rows are null; `records()` gives every row at once. Numbers are\nJavaScript numbers, except 64-bit integer columns, which read as `bigint` (and as\n`BigInt64Array` or `BigUint64Array` columns)."},"819":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-61","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (a `Uint8Array`), read in place by `Reader`; iterating\ngives each row as a plain object, with `null` where a value is null. `init()` loads and compiles\nthe module once; call it before the first `Engine`:"},"820":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-62","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Whole columns read faster for large results: `reader.numbers(\"lat\")` is a typed array viewing\nthe blob, `reader.strings(\"name\")` an array. 64-bit integer columns read as `bigint`."},"821":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-63","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (`Data`): the rows as compact columns, read in place.\n`Reader` reads it, and iterating gives a `Record` per row, with typed accessors that return\n`nil` where a value is null:"},"822":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-64","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"`place[\"level\"]` is a `Value` (`.int`, `.uint`, `.double`, `.string`) when you want the raw\nkind. For large results read whole columns: `reader.numbers(\"lat\")`, `reader.strings(\"name\")`,\n`reader.nulls(\"elevation\")`."},"823":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-65","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (`byte[]`): the rows as compact columns, read in place.\n`Reader` reads it; iterating gives each row as a `Map<String, Object>` in column order, with\n`null` where a value is null. Values are `String`, `Integer`, `Long`, `Float` or `Double`, by the\ncolumn's type:"},"824":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-66","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"For large results read whole columns: `numbers(\"lat\")` is a `double[]`, `strings(\"name\")` a\n`String[]`, `nulls(\"elevation\")` a `boolean[]`."},"825":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-67","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (`byte[]`): the rows as compact columns, read in place.\n`Reader` reads it; iterating gives each row as a `Map<String, Object>` in column order, with\n`null` where a value is null. Values are `String`, `Integer`, `Long`, `Float` or `Double`, by the\ncolumn's type:"},"826":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-68","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"For large results read whole columns: `numbers(\"lat\")` is a `double[]`, `strings(\"name\")` a\n`String[]`, `nulls(\"elevation\")` a `boolean[]`."},"827":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-69","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (`Uint8List`): the rows as compact columns, read in\nplace. `Reader` reads it; iterating gives each row as a `Map<String, Object?>`, with `null` where\na value is null. Dart records make the numbers easy to pull out:"},"828":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-70","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"`Reader(blob).single` takes the one row of a lookup. Whole columns: `numbers('lat')`,\n`strings('name')`, `nulls('elevation')`."},"829":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-71","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (`[]byte`), read in place by `NewReader`. `Records()`\ngives each row as a `Record` (a `map[string]any`, `nil` where null); the tour reads every\nresult through one helper:"},"830":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-72","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Typed column reads avoid the maps for large results: `r.Strings(\"name\")`,\n`tg.Values[float64](r, \"lat\")` (a slice viewing the blob, no copy), `r.Nulls(\"elevation\")`."},"831":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-73","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query writes a **result blob** into a `tg_bytes` you free with `tg_bytes_free`.\n`teigeodata_reader.h` (header-only, C99) reads it in place: `tg_reader_init` checks the layout\nonce, then `tg_reader_find` names a column and `tg_reader_string`, `tg_reader_number`,\n`tg_reader_int` and `tg_reader_is_null` read a row of it without allocating. The tour wraps them\nin a few helpers:"},"832":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-74","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"A `tg_str` points into the blob: copy it if you need it after `tg_bytes_free`."},"833":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-75","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a `tg::Blob` (the result's bytes, freed when it goes out of scope).\n`tg::Reader` reads it in place; iterating gives a `Row` per row with `string`, `number` and\n`integer` accessors that return `std::optional` (empty where null):"},"834":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-76","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Keep the `Blob` alive while you read it: the reader and every `string_view` borrow it, and a\n`Reader` cannot be made from a temporary. `records()` gives each row as a `std::map` of\n`std::variant` values; `numbers`, `strings` and `nulls` read whole columns."},"835":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-77","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"Every query returns a **result blob** (`byte[]`), read in place by `BlobReader`; iterating\ngives each row as an `IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object?>`, `null` where a value is null:"},"836":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-78","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#read-a-result","content":"For large results, `Values<double>(\"lat\")` is a `ReadOnlySpan<double>` over the blob itself, no\ncopy; `Strings(\"name\")` and `Nulls(\"elevation\")` read the rest."},"837":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-79","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#names-in-your-language","content":"The docs name calls as Rust, Python and C++ spell them (`tz_at`, `search_with`). Each\nbinding spells them in its language's style:"},"838":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-80","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#names-in-your-language","content":"snake_case · Rust, Python, C++ · tz_at, search_with","table":"table-0"},"839":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-81","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#names-in-your-language","content":"camelCase · Node.js, Browser, Swift, Kotlin, Java, Dart · tzAt, searchWith","table":"table-0"},"840":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-82","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#names-in-your-language","content":"PascalCase · Go, C# · TzAt, SearchWith","table":"table-0"},"841":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-83","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#names-in-your-language","content":"tg_engine_ prefix · C · tg_engine_tz_at, tg_engine_search_with","table":"table-0"},"842":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-84","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#next","content":"Open a pack, then search."},"843":{"id":"/docs/getting-started/install-85","page_id":"/docs/getting-started/install","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started/install#next","content":"Use cases: a search box, a GPS fix to an address, local time anywhere."},"844":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","type":"page","content":"Admin units at a point","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/admin"},"845":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin","content":"Every admin unit that holds a point, country first, and countries and subdivisions by their ISO 3166 codes."},"846":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/admin#next","content":"Next"},"847":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"848":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin","content":"`admin_at` tests the point against the admin boundaries and gives every unit that holds\nit, country first. `by_iso` finds a country or a subdivision by its ISO 3166 code (`NP`,\n`NP-P4`, in either case)."},"849":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin","content":"Boundaries are exact polygons, so a point near a border is answered by the side it is on,\nnot by the nearest centre. Disputed boundaries are drawn from one stated perspective; see\naccuracy."},"850":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"851":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"852":{"id":"/docs/guides/admin-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/admin","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/admin#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"853":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","type":"page","content":"Outlines","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/boundaries"},"854":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"A unit's outline as GeoJSON at a level of detail, ready for MapLibre, Leaflet, OpenLayers, Shapely or Turf."},"855":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/boundaries#next","content":"Next"},"856":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"857":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"`boundary` answers a unit's outline as one row: `geojson`, an RFC 7946 Feature (a\nPolygon or MultiPolygon, outer rings counterclockwise and holes clockwise, seven decimals as\nstored), with the `lod` read, its `tolerance_m` and the number of `vertices`. A place with no\noutline answers its point as the geometry; an unknown ID answers no row."},"858":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"`Engine::boundary` decodes the outline into an `Outline` you reuse (its rings, points and\n`tolerance_cm`) and returns the layer that held it; `Outline::write_geojson` writes the same\nGeoJSON every binding answers. `Engine::boundary_vertices` counts vertices without decoding."},"859":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"The Feature goes to a map as it is: MapLibre's and Leaflet's GeoJSON sources, OpenLayers,\nShapely's `shape()`, Turf and JTS all read it."},"860":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"**Level of detail.** `lod` 0 is full resolution; 1 to 6 are coarser, each within its\n`tolerance_m`, with neighbouring units still sharing their borders at every level. A pack\nstored at full resolution only (the lite packs) answers every level as 0. A country at level 0\ncan be megabytes: draw a coarser level, and keep 0 for measuring."},"861":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"`lod` above 6 is `InvalidArgument`; a pack installed without `boundaries` is\n`MissingComponent`."},"862":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries","content":"Whether a point is *inside* a unit is `admin_at`, which tests the same outlines without\nbuilding them."},"863":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"864":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"865":{"id":"/docs/guides/boundaries-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/boundaries","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/boundaries#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"866":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","type":"page","content":"Distance and bearing","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/distance"},"867":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance","content":"The great-circle distance and initial bearing between two points or two places."},"868":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/distance#next","content":"Next"},"869":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"870":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance","content":"`distance_m` is the great-circle distance in metres (haversine on the WGS84 mean\nsphere, within about 0.5% of the ellipsoid), and `bearing_deg` the initial bearing from the\nfirst point to the second, in degrees clockwise from north. Neither needs a pack. Between two\nplaces you already have, `distance` takes their IDs and answers one row with `meters` and\n`bearing`."},"871":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance","content":"The free functions are `meters_between` and `bearing_deg`, taking degrees; between two\nplaces, pass their points."},"872":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance","content":"`distance` answers no row when either ID is unknown. From a point to a place, pass the\nplace's `lat` and `lon` to `distance_m`. The bearing is the direction you set off in: on a\ngreat circle it changes along the way, so the bearing back is not the reverse of this one."},"873":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"874":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"875":{"id":"/docs/guides/distance-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/distance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/distance#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"876":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","type":"page","content":"Elevation","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/elevation"},"877":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation","content":"The terrain height at any point, in metres above EGM2008, from the finest grid installed."},"878":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/elevation#next","content":"Next"},"879":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"880":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation","content":"`elevation_at` reads the terrain height at a point from the pack's elevation grid,\ninterpolated between the four samples around it: `meters` above EGM2008, the `source` product,\nthe grid's `resolution_m` and the `datum`. There is no row at sea or outside every grid."},"881":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation","content":"Every place also has its own `elevation` column. The world pack carries a coarse grid; a\ncountry's terrain pack adds a finer one, and the engine answers from the finest grid that covers\nthe point."},"882":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"883":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"884":{"id":"/docs/guides/elevation-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/elevation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/elevation#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"885":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","type":"page","content":"Errors","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/errors"},"886":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"The eight stable error codes, the same in every language, and how each language raises them."},"887":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"In your language"},"888":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"A call fails with one of eight stable codes, the same in every language:"},"889":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"InvalidArgument · An argument is outside its range: a latitude of 91, a limit over 10,000, a malformed ID.","table":"table-0"},"890":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"MissingComponent · The query needs a component the pack was installed without (search on a pack installed without search).","table":"table-0"},"891":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"Corrupt · The bytes are not a valid pack, or a section does not match its hash.","table":"table-0"},"892":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"Io · A file could not be read.","table":"table-0"},"893":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"Network · No mirror answered, or the connection broke (the store only).","table":"table-0"},"894":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"Refused · The store refused: the catalog did not verify, a pack is not installed or another needs it.","table":"table-0"},"895":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"Cancelled · A callback or a job was asked to stop.","table":"table-0"},"896":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"Internal · A bug inside teigeodata; a panic never crosses into your program.","table":"table-0"},"897":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors","content":"A query that finds nothing is not an error: it returns no rows."},"898":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Rust returns `Result`s: the tour's `main` returns `Result<(), Box<dyn Error>>` and uses `?`\nthroughout. A point is checked when it is made, so the latitude error comes from\n`Point::from_degrees`:"},"899":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"A query fails with the engine's `Error`; the bindings map it to their eight codes:"},"900":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Io · A file could not be read. · Io","table":"table-1"},"901":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Format · The bytes are not a valid pack, or a section's structure is broken. · Corrupt","table":"table-1"},"902":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Integrity { address, kind } · A section's bytes do not match its hash. · Corrupt","table":"table-1"},"903":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Range · A section is missing or a byte range is outside it. · Corrupt","table":"table-1"},"904":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"MissingComponent { address, component } · The query needs a component the layer was installed without. · MissingComponent","table":"table-1"},"905":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Arguments are checked by their types before a query runs: a `Point` is in range when it is made,\nand a `PlaceId` or an `IsoCode` when it is parsed. The store (`teigeodata-sync`) has its own\nerror type for the network and the catalog."},"906":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Each code is an exception class under `teigeodata.TeiGeodataError`:\n`InvalidArgumentError`, `MissingComponentError`, `CorruptError` and so on."},"907":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Errors are thrown as `Error`s with a `code` property, one of the names above:"},"908":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Errors are thrown as `Error`s with a `code` property, one of the names above:"},"909":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Calls throw `TeiGeodataError` (typed throws), whose `code` is one of the names above\n(`.invalidArgument`, `.missingComponent`, …) and whose `message` says what was wrong:"},"910":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Each code is a subclass of the unchecked `TeiGeodataException`:\n`TeiGeodataException.InvalidArgument`, `.MissingComponent` and so on."},"911":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Each code is a subclass of the unchecked `TeiGeodataException`:\n`TeiGeodataException.InvalidArgument`, `.MissingComponent` and so on."},"912":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Each code is a subclass of `TeiGeodataException`: `InvalidArgumentException`,\n`MissingComponentException` and so on:"},"913":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Every failure is a `*tg.Error` with a stable `Code`; match it with `errors.As`, or\n`errors.Is(err, tg.CodeMissingComponent)`:"},"914":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Every function returns `TG_OK` (0) or an error code (`TG_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT`, …);\n`tg_last_error` gives the calling thread's message:"},"915":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Calls throw `teigeodata::Error`, whose `code()` is a `teigeodata::ErrorCode`:"},"916":{"id":"/docs/guides/errors-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/errors","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/errors#in-your-language","content":"Calls throw `TeiGeodataException`, whose `Code` is an `ErrorCode`:"},"917":{"id":"/docs/guides/examples","page_id":"/docs/guides/examples","type":"page","content":"The example programs","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/examples"},"918":{"id":"/docs/guides/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/examples","content":"The whole program behind every snippet in these docs, in each language, and how to run it."},"919":{"id":"/docs/guides/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/examples","content":"Every snippet in these docs is a region of one program per language. CI runs each on the\nreleased pack and compares its output line by line. Run one from a checkout, which builds what\nthe language needs and fetches the pinned pack:"},"920":{"id":"/docs/guides/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/examples","content":"The output, the same for every language:"},"921":{"id":"/docs/guides/open","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","type":"page","content":"Open a pack","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/open"},"922":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"Open a pack from a file or from bytes, add and remove layers, and choose what is verified and read."},"923":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/open#next","content":"Next"},"924":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"925":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"An `Engine` holds the packs it answers from. `open_file` memory-maps a pack file and\nadds it as a **layer**; the engine answers from every layer at once, and a higher layer wins\nwhere two disagree. Opening is cheap: the head of the file is read and checked and the block hashes of large\nsections are proved; each section is verified against its hash the first time a query reads\nit."},"926":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"`Layer::open` maps the file and checks its head; `Engine::add` adds the layer and returns\nits `LayerId`. `Layer::from_bytes` and `Layer::open_range` open bytes in memory and a pack inside\nanother file; `LayerOptions` holds the priority, `Verify`, `Load` and the parts to read."},"927":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"`Engine::remove` takes a layer out by its `LayerId`. For layers that come and go while\nother threads query, see `Shared` in threads."},"928":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"A page has no file system: `engine.openBytes(bytes)` opens a pack from a `Uint8Array` (a\ndownload, an asset, a file the user picked), copying it into the module's memory once.\n`openFile`, the store and the `…Async` forms are left out of this package."},"929":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"`open_bytes` adds a pack from bytes already in memory (a download, an app asset, the\nbrowser). `open_file_range` opens a pack stored uncompressed inside another file, such as an\nAndroid APK."},"930":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"Each `_with` form takes `OpenOptions`: precedence over the other packs, when sections are\nverified (lazy, eager or trusted), whether the file is mapped or read, and which components,\nlanguages and outline level to read."},"931":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open","content":"`open_file` returns the layer's number; `remove` takes it out again while other threads\nkeep querying. One engine answers from any number of threads at once."},"932":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"933":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"934":{"id":"/docs/guides/open-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/open","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/open#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"935":{"id":"/docs/guides/places","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","type":"page","content":"Places and the hierarchy","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/places"},"936":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places","content":"Look a place up by its stable ID, and walk its admin units up to the country and down to its children."},"937":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/places#get-a-place-by-its-id","content":"Get a place by its ID"},"938":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/places#walk-the-admin-hierarchy","content":"Walk the admin hierarchy"},"939":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/places#next","content":"Next"},"940":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"941":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#get-a-place-by-its-id","content":"Every place has a stable ID such as `tgd:np/1xhb`: the country, then a short code. IDs\nsurvive rebuilds of the data; when two places are merged, `get` on the retired ID answers\nwith the survivor, so IDs you stored keep working."},"942":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#get-a-place-by-its-id","content":"IDs parse into a `PlaceId`. A `Place` holds its ID, kind, level and point; read the rest on\ndemand: `Engine::elevation`, `Engine::tz_of`, `Engine::parent`, each an `Option`."},"943":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#get-a-place-by-its-id","content":"`elevation` is metres above mean sea level (EGM2008), `tz` the IANA zone and `parent` the\nID of the unit that holds the place. Any column may be null where the data has no value: read\neach through the reader, which gives your language's null."},"944":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#walk-the-admin-hierarchy","content":"`ancestors` lists the units that hold a place, parent first, up to its country; `parent`\ngives the first of them alone and `children` the units and places one level down."},"945":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#walk-the-admin-hierarchy","content":"`level` is the admin level as each country defines it: 0 is a country (and a settlement,\nwhich is not a unit), 1 a province or state, and so on down to the lowest level the data has.\nEach country's ladder is in its profile on the data pages."},"946":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"947":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"948":{"id":"/docs/guides/places-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/places","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/places#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"949":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","type":"page","content":"Reverse geocoding","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/reverse"},"950":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse","content":"From a point to the places near it: the nearest few, everything within a radius, and the places around a place."},"951":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/reverse#reverse-geocode-a-point","content":"Reverse geocode a point"},"952":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/reverse#find-everything-within-a-radius","content":"Find everything within a radius"},"953":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/reverse#find-places-near-a-place","content":"Find places near a place"},"954":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/reverse#next","content":"Next"},"955":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse","content":"Three ways to ask what is near, each nearest first with its distance and bearing:"},"956":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"957":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#reverse-geocode-a-point","content":"`reverse` gives the places nearest a point, nearest first, each with `meters` (the\ngreat-circle distance) and `bearing` (degrees clockwise from north, from the place to the\npoint: \"3.2 km at 45° from Pokhara\")."},"958":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#reverse-geocode-a-point","content":"Results are `Near` values: the `place`, `meters` and `bearing_deg`, written into a `Vec`\nyou reuse."},"959":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#reverse-geocode-a-point","content":"For the admin units that *hold* a point (its district, province and country) use\n`admin_at` below; reverse answers with what is *near* it."},"960":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#find-everything-within-a-radius","content":"`within_radius` gives the places within a distance of a point, nearest first, with the\nsame `meters` and `bearing` columns, at most `limit` of them."},"961":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#find-places-near-a-place","content":"`nearby` is reverse geocoding from a place you already have: the places nearest it,\nitself left out."},"962":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"963":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"964":{"id":"/docs/guides/reverse-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/reverse","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/reverse#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"965":{"id":"/docs/guides/search","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","type":"page","content":"Search by name","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/search"},"966":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search","content":"Find places by name in every script, narrow by parent, rank by distance from a point, and tune an autocomplete."},"967":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/search#search-by-name","content":"Search by name"},"968":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/search#narrow-a-search-by-its-parent","content":"Narrow a search by its parent"},"969":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/search#rank-by-distance-from-a-point","content":"Rank by distance from a point"},"970":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/search#next","content":"Next"},"971":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search","content":"Search finds places by any of their names, forgives spelling, and ranks the right place first. Three forms, from the simplest:"},"972":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"973":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#search-by-name","content":"`search` finds places by name, best first. It matches every name a place has, in every\nscript, and forgives spelling: काठमाडौं, Kathmandu and Kathamandu find the same city, and so do\ntypos, missing accents and abbreviations. A result row has the place columns (`id`, `name`,\n`kind`, `level`, `lat`, `lon`, `elevation`, `parent`, `tz`, `iso`, `population`,\n`population_kind`) and a `score`."},"974":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#search-by-name","content":"`Engine::search_with` writes `Hit`s (a `Place` and its `score`) into a `Vec` you reuse,\nwith a `Scratch` for its working memory, so a warm search allocates nothing. The engine takes\nany `k`; the bindings cap `limit` at `LIMIT_MAX` in `teigeodata-api`."},"975":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#search-by-name","content":"The `score` ranks the rows of one query and means nothing across queries. It adds how well the\nname matched to how important the place is, so a city ranks above a village of the same name.\n`limit` is at most 10,000 (`LIMIT_MAX`)."},"976":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#narrow-a-search-by-its-parent","content":"Write the place's parent after a comma, as people do: `\"Kathmandu, Bagmati\"`. Places\ninside a unit of that name rank first; the parent can be any ancestor (a district, a province,\na country) and is matched with the same tolerance as the name."},"977":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#narrow-a-search-by-its-parent","content":"The city (a settlement, level 0) and the metropolitan municipality (an admin unit at\nlevel 3) share the name, and both are in Bagmati."},"978":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#rank-by-distance-from-a-point","content":"Many places share a name. `search_with` takes `SearchOptions`: a **focus** point (the\nuser's location, or the middle of the map they are looking at) ranks near places first. A place\nat the focus gains 30 points, one `focus_scale_m` away 15, and the gain keeps halving with\ndistance (the default scale is 50 km)."},"979":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#rank-by-distance-from-a-point","content":"The focus is `SearchOptions::with_focus(Focus { at, scale_m })`; `SearchOptions::strict`\nsets the strict order."},"980":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#rank-by-distance-from-a-point","content":"Set `strict` to rank by how well the name matched first (exact, loose, prefix, one edit,\nsubstring, two edits, phonetic), then by importance and focus, instead of one combined score:\nuseful for an autocomplete that must show exact matches on top."},"981":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"982":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"983":{"id":"/docs/guides/search-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/search","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/search#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"984":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","type":"page","content":"Threads and platforms","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/threads"},"985":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Sharing an engine between threads, async calls, and what each platform needs: Android assets, Flutter, the browser."},"986":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"One engine answers from any number of threads at once, and opening a pack maps it once\nfor all of them. What changes by language is how you share it and how the store's long calls\n(download, install, update) run without blocking:"},"987":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads and updates**"},"988":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"`Engine` is `Send + Sync`: share it in an `Arc` and query from any number of threads. To add or\nremove layers while other threads query, hold a `Shared`: `Shared::load` gives the snapshot a\nquery runs on, and `Shared::update` changes a copy and publishes it atomically, so queries\nalready running finish on the snapshot they started with:"},"989":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads and asyncio**"},"990":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"An `Engine` is safe to share between threads, and queries release the GIL: a thread pool runs\nthem in parallel, and on a free-threaded build so does every thread. The store's long calls\n(install, update) run as a `Job`; `teigeodata.aio` awaits one (`await aio.result(job)`) and\nstreams its progress (`async for done, total in aio.progress(job)`)."},"991":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads and the event loop**"},"992":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Queries are synchronous and fast (microseconds), so they run on the calling thread. The store's\nlong calls have `…Async` forms that return promises and run off the event loop\n(`await store.catalogAsync()`), and a `Job` reports its progress while it downloads. Worker\nthreads can each open their own `Engine` on the same file: the pages are mapped once and shared."},"993":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**In a page**"},"994":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Cache the pack.** Serve it with a long `Cache-Control`, or keep it in the Cache API or\nOPFS so later visits work offline."},"995":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Run it in a Worker** when the pack is large: opening copies the bytes into the module's\nmemory once; queries then take microseconds and never block on the network."},"996":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Bundlers:** the package's `exports` pick the right entry for browsers, Node.js, Deno and\n`workerd` (Cloudflare Workers); Vite, webpack and esbuild handle the `.wasm` file as an asset."},"997":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Concurrency**"},"998":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"`Engine`, `Store` and `Job` are `Sendable`: share one engine between tasks and threads. Queries\nare fast enough for the main actor; the store's blocking calls have `async` forms\n(`try await store.catalogAsync()`), and a `Job` streams its progress as an `AsyncSequence`."},"999":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Bundling a pack**"},"1000":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Add the `.tgd` file to the app's resources and open it in place:\n`try engine.openFile(Bundle.main.path(forResource: \"world-np-lite\", ofType: \"tgd\")!)`. The file is\nmemory-mapped, so it costs no heap."},"1001":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads, coroutines and Android**"},"1002":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Handles are thread-safe and `AutoCloseable` (`use { }` in Kotlin, try-with-resources in Java);\na cleaner frees one you forget. The store's blocking calls have `…Async` forms returning\n`CompletableFuture`, which Kotlin awaits with `kotlinx-coroutines-jdk8`'s `await()`."},"1003":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"On Android, keep a bundled pack uncompressed (`androidResources { noCompress += \"tgd\" }`) and\nopen it in place, without copying it out of the APK:"},"1004":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads, coroutines and Android**"},"1005":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Handles are thread-safe and `AutoCloseable` (`use { }` in Kotlin, try-with-resources in Java);\na cleaner frees one you forget. The store's blocking calls have `…Async` forms returning\n`CompletableFuture`, which Kotlin awaits with `kotlinx-coroutines-jdk8`'s `await()`."},"1006":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"On Android, keep a bundled pack uncompressed (`androidResources { noCompress += \"tgd\" }`) and\nopen it in place, without copying it out of the APK:"},"1007":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Flutter**"},"1008":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Await `TeiGeodata.init()` once** before the first engine. Natively it returns at once; on\nthe web it loads the WebAssembly module, whose files go in `web/teigeodata_wasm/`."},"1009":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Bundle a pack** as an asset and open it from its bytes\n(`engine.openBytes((await rootBundle.load('assets/world-np-lite.tgd')).buffer.asUint8List())`),\nor copy it to the app's documents directory once and `openFile` it there to map it instead."},"1010":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Isolates.** Queries take microseconds and are fine on the UI isolate. The store's blocking\ncalls have `…Async` forms that run on another thread, and a `Job` streams its progress."},"1011":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"`cargo xtask flutter-check` runs the package's integration test in a Flutter app on the\ndesktop, an iOS simulator or an Android device."},"1012":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Goroutines**"},"1013":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Handles may be used from any goroutine at once; `Close` frees one once no call holds it. The\nstore's long calls run as a `Job` whose progress you poll or wait for (`WaitProgress`) and which\n`Cancel` stops."},"1014":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads and memory**"},"1015":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"Handles are thread-safe: any number of threads may query one `tg_engine` at once. Every\n`tg_bytes` the library returns is freed with `tg_bytes_free`, every handle with its `_free`; a\npanic inside the library is caught at the boundary and returned as `TG_ERROR_INTERNAL`."},"1016":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads**"},"1017":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"An `Engine` answers from any number of threads at once. Handles are move-only."},"1018":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"**Threads and async**"},"1019":{"id":"/docs/guides/threads-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/threads","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/threads","content":"An `Engine` answers from any number of threads; `Dispose` frees it once no call holds it. The\nstore's blocking calls have awaitable forms (`Store.OpenAsync`, `CatalogAsync`, `StatusAsync`,\n`RemoveAsync`, …), and a `Job` streams its progress with `ProgressAsync`, gives its result with\n`ResultAsync` and cancels through a `CancellationToken`."},"1020":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","type":"page","content":"Timezones","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/timezones"},"1021":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones","content":"The IANA zone at a point, its UTC offset at an instant, and whether a local time exists, from the tzdb rules in the pack."},"1022":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/timezones#next","content":"Next"},"1023":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones","content":"Each snippet is cut from one program per language, the tour,\nwhich CI runs on the released pack `world-np-lite.tgd` (`data-2026.11.14.3`) and checks line by\nline against the output shown. The output is the same in every language. Pick your language\nonce: every page remembers it."},"1024":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones","content":"`tz_at` gives the IANA zone at a point as the zone boundaries draw it. With the zone,\n`tz_offset_at` gives the UTC offset at an instant (from the tzdb rules inside the pack, not\nfrom the operating system), and `tz_local` tells whether a wall-clock time exists: `unique`,\n`gap` (skipped by a daylight-saving change) or `overlap` (it happens twice), with the offsets\nbefore and after."},"1025":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones","content":"`tz_at` answers the timezone area as a `Place`; `Engine::zone_name` reads its IANA name.\nLocal times are `Engine::tz_local_candidates`: `Local::Unique`, `Local::Gap` or\n`Local::Overlap`."},"1026":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones","content":"`tz_version` names the tzdb release the rules come from, so you can tell when a pack is\nolder than a rule change; `tz_zones` lists every zone the packs hold."},"1027":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones#next","content":"Reference: every query, its columns and its errors."},"1028":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones#next","content":"Errors: the eight codes and how each language raises them."},"1029":{"id":"/docs/guides/timezones-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/timezones","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/timezones#next","content":"Concepts: places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store."},"1030":{"id":"/docs","page_id":"/docs","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs"},"1031":{"id":"/docs-0","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"What teigeodata is, what it answers, and where to start."},"1032":{"id":"/docs-1","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#one-engine-every-language","content":"One engine, every language"},"1033":{"id":"/docs-2","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"Where to start"},"1034":{"id":"/docs-3","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#status","content":"Status"},"1035":{"id":"/docs-4","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"teigeodata answers questions about places, on the device, with no network and no API key:"},"1036":{"id":"/docs-5","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**Search by name** in any script and spelling: काठमाडौं, Kathmandu and Kathamandu find the\nsame city; `\"Kathmandu, Bagmati\"` ranks the one in Bagmati first; a focus point ranks what is\nnear the user."},"1037":{"id":"/docs-6","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**Reverse geocode** a point to the nearest places, with distance and bearing, and to every\nadmin unit that holds it, from the country down to the lowest level the data has."},"1038":{"id":"/docs-7","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**Timezones**: the IANA zone at any point as its boundaries draw it, the UTC offset at any\ninstant from tzdb rules carried in the pack, and whether a local time is skipped or repeated."},"1039":{"id":"/docs-8","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**Elevation** of every place, and the terrain height at any point."},"1040":{"id":"/docs-9","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**The hierarchy, ISO 3166 codes, postal codes** and stable IDs that survive data updates."},"1041":{"id":"/docs-10","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"The data is cleaned, deduplicated and merged from the best open sources, and ships as **packs**:\ncompact, memory-mapped files, every section hashed and checked as it is read (and signed, from\nthe first catalog release). Bundle one in your app, or download a country when a user\nneeds it. Every pack credits its sources, and you may keep and redistribute what it answers."},"1042":{"id":"/docs-11","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"To try it before installing anything, open the playground: the WebAssembly\nengine and a released pack in your browser, every query with the code that produced it."},"1043":{"id":"/docs-12","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#one-engine-every-language","content":"The engine is written in Rust. Rust, C, C++, Swift, Kotlin, Java, Dart, Python, Node.js, the\nbrowser (WebAssembly), Go and .NET call it through bindings generated from one description of\nthe API, so each query, option and error code is the same everywhere. Every guide shows each\nlanguage in a tab: pick yours once and every page remembers it."},"1044":{"id":"/docs-13","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#one-engine-every-language","content":"There is also a command line, an HTTP server (native JSON,\nGeoJSON and Nominatim-compatible routes) and an MCP server that gives AI\nagents the same queries as tools."},"1045":{"id":"/docs-14","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"<Card title=\"Quickstart\" href=\"/docs/getting-started\" description=\"A pack and your first answers in five minutes.\" />"},"1046":{"id":"/docs-15","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"<Card title=\"Install\" href=\"/docs/getting-started/install\" description=\"Add the package in your language and read a result.\" />"},"1047":{"id":"/docs-16","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"<Card title=\"Use cases\" href=\"/docs/tutorials\" description=\"Step by step: a search box, a GPS fix to an address, local time anywhere, offline downloads.\" />"},"1048":{"id":"/docs-17","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"<Card title=\"Guides\" href=\"/docs/guides/search\" description=\"Every query, task by task, in every language.\" />"},"1049":{"id":"/docs-18","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"<Card title=\"Concepts\" href=\"/docs/concepts\" description=\"Places and IDs, packs and components, results, the store, accuracy.\" />"},"1050":{"id":"/docs-19","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#where-to-start","content":"<Card title=\"Reference\" href=\"/docs/reference\" description=\"Every call, its arguments, columns and errors.\" />"},"1051":{"id":"/docs-20","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#status","content":"The code is at 1.1, on every package registry, and the data is published as pre-releases (the\nlatest is `data-2026.11.15`, Nepal's main and lite packs with populations). Nepal is covered in full, with the world's countries, first-level units\nand cities of 5,000 or more, and India's districts and towns; coverage grows by data releases,\nIndia next. The packages are on crates.io, PyPI, npm, Maven Central, pub.dev and the Go module\nproxy; Swift installs from the release tags and C from the release archives\n(install). The .NET package is not on NuGet yet."},"1052":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"page","content":"Engine","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine"},"1053":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine","content":"Open packs answering place queries."},"1054":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#new","content":"`new`"},"1055":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file","content":"`open_file`"},"1056":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-3","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_with","content":"`open_file_with`"},"1057":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-4","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_range","content":"`open_file_range`"},"1058":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-5","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_range_with","content":"`open_file_range_with`"},"1059":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-6","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_bytes","content":"`open_bytes`"},"1060":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-7","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_bytes_with","content":"`open_bytes_with`"},"1061":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-8","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#remove","content":"`remove`"},"1062":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-9","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#layers","content":"`layers`"},"1063":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-10","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#get","content":"`get`"},"1064":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-11","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#parent","content":"`parent`"},"1065":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-12","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#ancestors","content":"`ancestors`"},"1066":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-13","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#children","content":"`children`"},"1067":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-14","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#nearby","content":"`nearby`"},"1068":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-15","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#search","content":"`search`"},"1069":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-16","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#search_with","content":"`search_with`"},"1070":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-17","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#reverse","content":"`reverse`"},"1071":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-18","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#within_radius","content":"`within_radius`"},"1072":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-19","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#admin_at","content":"`admin_at`"},"1073":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-20","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#elevation_at","content":"`elevation_at`"},"1074":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-21","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_lookup","content":"`postal_lookup`"},"1075":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-22","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_prefix","content":"`postal_prefix`"},"1076":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-23","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_codes_of","content":"`postal_codes_of`"},"1077":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-24","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_at","content":"`postal_at`"},"1078":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-25","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_at","content":"`tz_at`"},"1079":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-26","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_offset_at","content":"`tz_offset_at`"},"1080":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-27","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_local","content":"`tz_local`"},"1081":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-28","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_zones","content":"`tz_zones`"},"1082":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-29","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_rules_start","content":"`tz_rules_start`"},"1083":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-30","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_version","content":"`tz_version`"},"1084":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-31","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#by_iso","content":"`by_iso`"},"1085":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-32","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#boundary","content":"`boundary`"},"1086":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-33","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/engine#distance","content":"`distance`"},"1087":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-34","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine","content":"Open packs answering place queries. Every method takes `&self`: one engine answers from any number of threads at once, and adding a pack publishes a new snapshot while queries go on."},"1088":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-35","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine","content":"Place results are blobs with the columns `id`, `name`, `kind`, `level`, `lat`, `lon`, `elevation`, `parent`, `tz`, `iso`, `population` and `population_kind` (`stated` or `modelled`; absent values are null), plus `score` for a search and `meters` and `bearing` for a reverse lookup."},"1089":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-36","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine","content":"Every query returns a result blob; read it with your language's\n`Reader`. Pick a language in any tab and every tab on the site follows."},"1090":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-37","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#new","content":"An engine with no packs."},"1091":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-38","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file","content":"Opens the pack at `path` (memory-mapped) and adds it. Returns its layer ID, for `remove`: unique in this engine, counting up from 0."},"1092":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-39","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::Io` if the file cannot be read, `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if it is not a valid pack."},"1093":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-40","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_with","content":"Opens the pack at `path` with these options and adds it. Returns its layer ID, as `open_file`."},"1094":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-41","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_with","content":"**Errors.** As `open_file`, and `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for an outline level above 255."},"1095":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-42","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_range","content":"Opens the pack stored whole in `length` bytes at `offset` of the file at `path` (memory-mapped) and adds it: a pack bundled as an uncompressed asset of an Android app (`noCompress`; the path is the APK, the offset and length its `AssetFileDescriptor`'s), or one inside any container that stores it uncompressed. The offset need not be aligned. Returns its layer ID, as `open_file`."},"1096":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-43","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_range","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::Io` if the file cannot be read or the range is not inside it, `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if the range is not a valid pack."},"1097":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-44","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_range_with","content":"As `open_file_range`, with these options."},"1098":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-45","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_file_range_with","content":"**Errors.** As `open_file_range`, and `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for an outline level above 255."},"1099":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-46","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_bytes","content":"Adds a pack from its bytes (copied). Returns its layer ID, as `open_file`."},"1100":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-47","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_bytes","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if the bytes are not a valid pack."},"1101":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-48","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_bytes_with","content":"Adds a pack from its bytes (copied) with these options. Returns its layer ID, as `open_file`."},"1102":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-49","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#open_bytes_with","content":"**Errors.** As `open_bytes`, and `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for an outline level above 255."},"1103":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-50","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#remove","content":"Removes the pack with this layer ID; false if no pack has it. Queries already running finish on the snapshot they started with; the pack's memory is released after them."},"1104":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-51","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#layers","content":"The number of packs."},"1105":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-52","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#get","content":"The place with this ID (`tgd:np/1`), or no row; a retired ID gives its survivor."},"1106":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-53","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#get","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `id` is not a place ID; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1107":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-54","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#parent","content":"The place's parent as the packs answer for it (one row), or no row for a root or an unknown ID."},"1108":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-55","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#parent","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `id` is not a place ID; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1109":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-56","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#ancestors","content":"The place's ancestors, parent first up to the country; no rows for a root or an unknown ID."},"1110":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-57","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#ancestors","content":"**Errors.** As `parent`."},"1111":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-58","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#children","content":"The place's children, by ID; no rows for a leaf or an unknown ID."},"1112":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-59","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#children","content":"**Errors.** As `parent`."},"1113":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-60","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#nearby","content":"The places nearest the place with this ID, not counting it, nearest first, at most `limit`, with `meters` and `bearing` (from each place to it); no rows for an unknown ID."},"1114":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-61","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#nearby","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `id` is not a place ID or `limit` exceeds `LIMIT_MAX`; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1115":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-62","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#search","content":"Places matching `query`, best first, at most `limit`; \"Name, Parent\" ranks a named parent first. The `score` column compares only within one query."},"1116":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-63","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#search","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `limit` exceeds `LIMIT_MAX`; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1117":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-64","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#search_with","content":"`search` ranked by `options`: near a focus point, or in strict match order."},"1118":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-65","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#search_with","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `limit` exceeds `LIMIT_MAX`, the focus is off the globe or has one coordinate only, or its scale is not positive; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1119":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-66","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#reverse","content":"The places nearest the point, nearest first, at most `limit`, with `meters` and `bearing` (degrees clockwise from north, from each place to the point)."},"1120":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-67","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#reverse","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a point off the globe or a `limit` above `LIMIT_MAX`; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1121":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-68","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#within_radius","content":"The places within `radius_m` metres of the point (great-circle), nearest first, at most `limit`, with `meters` and `bearing` (from each place to the point)."},"1122":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-69","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#within_radius","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a point off the globe, a negative or non-finite radius, or a `limit` above `LIMIT_MAX`; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1123":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-70","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#admin_at","content":"The admin units holding the point, country first."},"1124":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-71","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#admin_at","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a point off the globe; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1125":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-72","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#elevation_at","content":"The terrain height at the point, as one row: `meters` above the datum (bilinear between the DEM samples around it), `source` (the DEM product's key, null where the pack does not state it), `resolution_m` (the sample spacing north to south) and `datum` (`EGM2008`). No row where no pack has terrain there (the sea, outside every grid)."},"1126":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-73","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#elevation_at","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a point off the globe; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a grid read is corrupt."},"1127":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-74","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_lookup","content":"The places postal code `code` names, one row per code and place, with the code's `postal`, `scheme`, `code_kind`, `valid_from` and `valid_to` columns. `scheme` names one scheme (`np`, `gb`); empty means every scheme whose rule accepts the input. The code may be typed with or without separators, in any case, or in another script's digits. Retired codes are returned too, with their validity."},"1128":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-75","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_lookup","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1129":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-76","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_prefix","content":"Up to `limit` postal codes starting with `prefix`, with their places (autocomplete), in code order per scheme; `scheme` as in `postal_lookup`."},"1130":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-77","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_prefix","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `limit` exceeds `LIMIT_MAX`; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1131":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-78","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_codes_of","content":"The postal codes of the place with this ID; no rows for an unknown ID."},"1132":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-79","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_codes_of","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `id` is not a place ID; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1133":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-80","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_at","content":"The postal codes of the point: those of the deepest admin unit holding it that an admin-aligned scheme gives codes to, with its parts (a ward's code; the point is in one of them). No rows where no unit holding the point has such codes."},"1134":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-81","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#postal_at","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a point off the globe; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1135":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-82","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_at","content":"The time-zone area holding the point (one row; its `tz` column is the IANA zone), or no row where no pack answers."},"1136":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-83","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_at","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a point off the globe; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1137":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-84","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_offset_at","content":"The UTC offset in force in `zone` (a tzdb name or link, `Asia/Kathmandu`) at `unix_seconds`, from the first layer whose zone rules hold the zone: one row with `seconds` east of UTC, `dst` (1 when tzdb counts it as daylight saving time) and `abbr` (`+0545`, `EDT`); no row where no layer holds the zone."},"1138":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-85","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_offset_at","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for an instant outside the years ±9,999; `ErrorCode::MissingComponent` if the rules that would hold the zone were not installed; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if they are corrupt."},"1139":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-86","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_local","content":"The offsets a civil time may have in `zone`: one row with `kind` (`unique`, `gap` for a time a transition skips, `overlap` for one that happens twice), `before` and `after` in seconds east of UTC (the offsets before and after the transition: for an overlap the earlier and the later instant's; for a unique time both the same); no row where no layer holds the zone."},"1140":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-87","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_local","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` for a date that is not one of the years ±9,999 or a time out of range; otherwise as `Engine::tz_offset_at`."},"1141":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-88","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_zones","content":"Every zone name (tzdb names and links) the layers' zone rules hold, in byte order: one `zone` column."},"1142":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-89","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_zones","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::MissingComponent` if no installed layer has rules and one has rules that were not installed."},"1143":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-90","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_rules_start","content":"The earliest instant `zone`'s rules cover, in Unix seconds: one `unix` row (its first transition, before which tzdb knows only local mean time; the year −9,999 for a zone that never changed); no row where no layer holds the zone."},"1144":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-91","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_rules_start","content":"**Errors.** As `Engine::tz_offset_at`."},"1145":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-92","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#tz_version","content":"The tzdb release (`2026f`) of the first layer with zone rules; empty without rules."},"1146":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-93","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#by_iso","content":"The place whose ISO 3166 code is `code` (`NP`, `NP-P3`, either case), or no row."},"1147":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-94","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#by_iso","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `code` is not an ISO 3166 code; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1148":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-95","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#boundary","content":"The outline of the place with this ID at level of detail `lod`, as one row: `id`, `lod` (the level read: 0 from a pack stored at full resolution only), `tolerance_m` (how far it may stray from the full-resolution outline), `vertices` and `geojson`, an RFC 7946 Feature ready for a map: a `Polygon` or `MultiPolygon` (outer rings counterclockwise, holes clockwise), or the place's `Point` when it has no outline. Level 0 is full resolution and 1 to `COARSEST_LOD` coarser; neighbours share their borders at every level. A whole country at level 0 can be megabytes: ask a coarser level to draw it small. No row for an unknown ID."},"1149":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-96","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#boundary","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `id` is not a place ID or `lod` exceeds `COARSEST_LOD`; `ErrorCode::MissingComponent` without `boundaries`; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1150":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-97","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#distance","content":"The great-circle distance between the places with these IDs, as one row: `meters` on the mean Earth sphere and `bearing`, the initial bearing from `from` to `to` in degrees clockwise from north. No row when either ID is unknown. From a point, use `distance_m` and `bearing_deg` with the place's `lat` and `lon`."},"1151":{"id":"/docs/reference/engine-98","page_id":"/docs/reference/engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/engine#distance","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if an ID is not a place ID; `ErrorCode::Corrupt` if a section read is corrupt."},"1152":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"page","content":"Functions","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions"},"1153":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions","content":"The library's free functions, outside any engine."},"1154":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#version","content":"`version`"},"1155":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#components","content":"`components`"},"1156":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-3","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#noop","content":"`noop`"},"1157":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-4","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#fold","content":"`fold`"},"1158":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-5","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#sample_rows","content":"`sample_rows`"},"1159":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-6","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#progress","content":"`progress`"},"1160":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-7","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#distance_m","content":"`distance_m`"},"1161":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-8","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/functions#bearing_deg","content":"`bearing_deg`"},"1162":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-9","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions","content":"These need no engine and no pack. `noop`, `sample_rows` and `progress` exist to measure and test\neach binding's call, result and callback paths; an application does not need them."},"1163":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-10","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#version","content":"The library version, from its package metadata."},"1164":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-11","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#components","content":"The components this build answers, in table order, comma-separated (`point, elevation, admin`). A build made without one answers its queries as a layer installed without it does: no rows, or `ErrorCode::MissingComponent`."},"1165":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-12","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#noop","content":"Does nothing. Measures the bare cost of a call across each boundary."},"1166":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-13","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#fold","content":"The search fold of a name: NFKD, lower case, optional accents dropped by script, spaces collapsed, recomposed to NFC. The same fold the forge and the search index use."},"1167":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-14","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#fold","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::MissingComponent` in a build without `search`, whose Unicode tables it uses."},"1168":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-15","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#sample_rows","content":"Builds `rows` deterministic sample places as a blob with the columns `id` (`u32`), `lat` and `lon` (`f64`, degrees), `elevation` (`f32`, metres) and `name` (UTF-8). Exercises the result path of every binding."},"1169":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-16","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#sample_rows","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if `rows` exceeds `SAMPLE_ROWS_MAX`."},"1170":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-17","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#progress","content":"Calls `on_step` once per step with the step number, from 1 to `steps`, on the calling thread, and returns the number of steps run. Exercises callbacks in every binding."},"1171":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-18","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#progress","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::Cancelled` if `on_step` returns `false`."},"1172":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-19","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#distance_m","content":"Great-circle distance in metres between two points in degrees, by the haversine formula on the mean Earth sphere (error up to about 0.5% against the ellipsoid), as every query measures it, with the same digits on every platform."},"1173":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-20","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#distance_m","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if a latitude is outside -90..=90, a longitude outside -180..=180, or any value is not finite."},"1174":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-21","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#bearing_deg","content":"The initial great-circle bearing from the first point to the second, in degrees clockwise from true north in `[0, 360)`; 0 for one point twice or from a pole. The same digits on every platform."},"1175":{"id":"/docs/reference/functions-22","page_id":"/docs/reference/functions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/functions#bearing_deg","content":"**Errors.** `ErrorCode::InvalidArgument` if a latitude is outside -90..=90, a longitude outside -180..=180, or any value is not finite."},"1176":{"id":"/docs/reference/http","page_id":"/docs/reference/http","type":"page","content":"HTTP API","breadcrumbs":["teigeodata","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/http"},"1177":{"id":"/docs/reference/http-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/http","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/http","content":"Every route of tgd serve with its parameters, generated from the server's route table."},"1178":{"id":"/docs/reference/http-1","page_id":"/docs/referen